# Line endings are a correctness issue in this repo, not a style preference.
#
# On Windows, `git config core.autocrlf true` is a common default (Git for Windows
# offers it during install). Without the rules below, a clone on such a host
# rewrites every text file to CRLF, and three separate things break:
#
#   * `scripts/*.sh` stops running at all. The shebang becomes
#     `#!/bin/sh\r`, and the kernel looks for an interpreter literally named
#     "sh\r". The error -- "bad interpreter: no such file or directory" -- names a
#     file that plainly exists, which is one of the least guessable failures in
#     Unix. This also bites WSL and Git Bash users on the same machine that
#     checked the file out.
#   * `scripts/test-render-parity.sh` fails. It compares rendered HTML against
#     `tests/golden/report.html` byte-for-byte, and a CRLF checkout of the golden
#     shifts every line by one byte.
#   * Credential-file mode checks read the wrong value. A trailing `\r` survives
#     the shell's `read`, so `FALCON_CLIENT_ID` ends with a carriage return and the
#     API rejects it with a 403 that looks like bad keys.
#
# So: LF in the working tree, on every platform, for everything executable or
# byte-compared. `eol=lf` is stronger than `text` alone -- it overrides
# core.autocrlf rather than deferring to it.

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# Files with no extension that are still shell or config.
env.example  text eol=lf
LICENSE      text eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never attempts any conversion or diff.
*.pptx  binary
*.docx  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.pdf   binary
